  [PASS] A1 non-shortest length prefix still carries a valid signature  -- 203 bytes, sha256 d5b1d222654fac88...
  [FINDING F3] (malleability) expected.statement_sha256 is not a signature-bound identity.
           RFC 9052 section 9 requires definite lengths and minimum-length arguments, but says the restriction applies to the Sig_structure, Enc_structure and MAC_structure -- not to the COSE message that carries them. So re-encoding the protected header's byte-string length in the two-byte form leaves the signed Sig_structure untouched and the signature still verifies, while the message becomes 203 bytes with a different SHA-256. The vector publishes statement_sha256 and statement_length_bytes as expectations, which treats the serialisation as an identity it is not: a verifier that matches on either must independently enforce RFC 8949 section 4.2.1, because nothing in COSE obliges the encoder to.
  [PASS] A1' a determinism-enforcing parse rejects it  -- which is the mitigation for F3
  [PASS] A2 tag 98 (COSE_Sign) is rejected  -- a Sign1 verifier must not accept a multi-signer structure
  [PASS] A3 untagged COSE_Sign1 is rejected by this verifier  -- accepted only where the content type establishes the type out of band
  [PASS] A4 63-byte signature is rejected on length, not on the curve  -- signature is 63 bytes, not 64
  [PASS] A5 S+L is rejected with the RFC 8032 range check
  [PASS] A5' the same bytes verify without it -- a second valid signature  -- why RFC 8032 section 5.1.7 requires 0 <= S < L
  [PASS] A6 duplicate map key in the protected header is rejected  -- RFC 8949 section 5.6
  [PASS] A7 payload stripped to nil cannot be verified as-is  -- no payload to place in the Sig_structure; must fail closed, not skip
  [PASS] A8 reading the payload as a CWT Claims Set contradicts the header  -- payload[1] is bytes, header iss is a text string
  [PASS] A9 a different chain head yields an identical CWT subject  -- sub is only the first 8 bytes of the head
  [FINDING F4] (design) The CWT subject truncates the chain head to 8 bytes.
           sub is 'pala-1:chain:' plus the first 16 hex characters of the head, so two different chains collide in the SCITT subject at a 64-bit birthday bound (~2^32 work). A transparency service that indexes or authorises by sub -- which is what sub is for -- cannot distinguish them. Verified here by signing a second statement over a head differing in byte 20: same sub, valid signature. The payload still commits to the full head, so this is an indexing and authorisation hazard rather than a forgery.
  [PASS] A10 an injected unprotected header does not disturb the signature  -- so a kid added later would be unauthenticated -- it belongs in protected
  [PASS] A11 trailing bytes after the statement are rejected  -- no silent truncation of the input

